# Check the Skill Index First — Including in Nested/External Repos

Before designing an approach or building tooling for a task, consult the skill
index in `AGENTS.md` and read the relevant `.cursor/skills/**/SKILL.md`. Skills
are plain markdown and encode live-verified platform behavior that is expensive
to rediscover.

## The failure mode this guards against

The skill index is always applied, so the usual way it gets missed is **not**
that an agent never saw it — it's that the work happened somewhere that felt
out of scope:

- A **nested clone of another repository** under this workspace (for example a
  third-party toolkit cloned in to be run against an org).
- A task framed around an **external tool**, a vendor repo, or a one-off script.
- A request phrased as *"deploy/run this repo"*, which reads as plumbing rather
  than as Revenue Cloud work.

None of those put the task outside the skills' scope. If the work touches
Revenue Cloud behavior — PST, quotes, orders, activation, pricing, bundles and
constraint models, assets, usage, invoicing, context, or the data model — the
skills apply regardless of which directory the code lives in.

## Rule

1. **Check before building.** When a task involves generating, converting, or
   activating Revenue Cloud transaction data, read
   `.cursor/skills/txn-data-harness/SKILL.md` (and, for backdated
   Quote→Order→Asset chains, `usage-consumption/building-usage-assets.md`)
   before writing new code. Overlapping tooling already exists.
2. **Say so when there is overlap.** If an existing skill or script covers what
   is being built, tell the user — even when they asked for a different tool.
   They may still choose the other tool; that is their call to make with the
   information, not a reason to withhold it.
3. **Reuse the hard-won contracts.** Skills document which endpoints are gone in
   the current API version, which fields are not writable, and which operations
   are blocked by platform validation. Prefer reading them over rediscovering
   the same walls empirically.

## Example

```
# ❌ BAD — treats a nested clone as a separate world
"The user asked me to run <vendor>/some-toolkit against the org, so I'll work
 entirely inside that directory and build the order-generation logic from
 scratch."

# ✅ GOOD
"This generates Revenue Cloud transaction data, so txn-data-harness is prior
 art even though the user asked for the vendor toolkit. Reading it first, and
 flagging the overlap."
```
